# examples/metronomus/: the timer, clock and multiplayer tick layer for Kiln
# and Punctim, driven by its probatio.exsc through every part of the library.
#
# expected.out (2,432 bytes) is WRITTEN BY prototypes/metronomus_oracle.py,
# not by this program: Python's // and %, fractions.Fraction for the clock,
# datetime for the civil dates, a dict for the input buffer. Regenerate it
# with
#   python3 prototypes/metronomus_oracle.py --punctim PUNCTIM_CHECKOUT \
#       tests/programs/metronomus/expected.out
# and `--punctim` re-reads Punctim's own game_vectors.json and
# snake_vectors.json and refuses to write if the embedded copies drifted.
#
# The exit status is the program's own check of Punctim's twelve golden
# vectors (four DCF-Game INPUT bodies, eight unwrap_pid cases): 0 only if all
# pass, 10+i / 20+i naming the first that does not.
#
# NON-VACUOUS, measured: of fifteen mechanical mutants of metronomus.exsc,
# every one that changes behaviour fails here, each at a stated byte
# (examples/metronomus/README.md lists them); the two that survived first
# were a missing boundary case and a missing tie, both added, and two more
# are equivalent mutants, said so there.
#
# cross=yes: the same bytes from the C backend's mips64-none-o64 row,
# big-endian, 32-bit addresses, under qemu -- the console's row.
TEST: expect-exit=0 sources=examples/metronomus/metronomus.exsc,examples/metronomus/probatio.exsc cross=yes
